OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Relative-generation-and-the-generator-problem-for-finite-factors-September-23-2026", "title": "Relative generation and the generator problem for finite factors", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Relative-generation-and-the-generator-problem-for-finite-factors-September-23-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "296", "family_title": "The generator problem for finite factors", "abstract_html": "We prove that every type II<sub>1</sub> factor with separable predual is generated by one operator, equivalently by two self-adjoint operators. Combined with the established direct-integral reduction to this case, this gives an affirmative solution of the generator problem for von Neumann algebras with separable predual.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\{u\\in\\mathcal U(M):W^*(P,u)=M\\} \\) is a dense \\(G_\\delta\\) subset of \\(\\mathcal U(M)\\) for the trace \\(2\\)-norm topology.", "statement": "<p>**Relative generation — original Theorem 1.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(P\\subset M\\) is an irreducible inclusion of type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) factors, and \\(M\\) has separable predual.</p><p class=\"li\">Irreducibility means \\(P'\\cap M=\\mathbb C1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(\\tau\\) for the normalized trace of \\(M\\), \\(\\|x\\|_2=\\tau(x^*x)^{1/2}\\), and \\(\\mathcal U(M)\\) for the unitary group.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(S\\subset M\\), \\(W^*(S)\\) is the smallest unital von Neumann subalgebra containing \\(S\\); adjoints are included, and closure is in the weak operator topology.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\{u\\in\\mathcal U(M):W^*(P,u)=M\\}\n\\]\nis a dense \\(G_\\delta\\) subset of \\(\\mathcal U(M)\\) for the trace \\(2\\)-norm topology.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 3 supplies a dense \\(G_\\delta\\) subset \\(\\mathcal C\\subset\\mathcal U(M)\\) consisting of relative generators. It remains to identify the full relative-generator locus as \\(G_\\delta\\).</p><p>Choose \\((\\eta_j)_{j\\geq1}\\) dense in \\(L^2(M,\\tau)\\). With\n\\[\nd_{\\eta_j}(v)\n=\\operatorname{dist}\\bigl(\\eta_j,L^2(W^*(P,v),\\tau)\\bigr),\n\\]\nthe full set of relative generators is\n\\[\n\\bigcap_{j,n\\geq1}\n\\{v\\in\\mathcal U(M):d_{\\eta_j}(v)&lt;1/n\\}.\n\\]\nEach set in the intersection is open by the upper semicontinuity established in Proposition 3. Vanishing of every distance is equivalent to\n\\[\nL^2(W^*(P,v),\\tau)=L^2(M,\\tau),\n\\]\nhence to \\(W^*(P,v)=M\\) by the trace-preserving conditional expectation. The set is dense because it contains \\(\\mathcal C\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\(M\\) is generated by two self-adjoint elements.", "statement": "<p>**Single generation of finite factors — original Theorem 1.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(M\\) is a type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) factor with separable predual.</p><p class=\"li\">Generation means unital von Neumann algebra generation.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\(M\\) is generated by two self-adjoint elements. Equivalently, there is an \\(x\\in M\\) such that\n\\[\nM=W^*(x).\n\\]\nThe equivalence uses \\(x=a+ib\\): its real and imaginary parts recover the self-adjoint elements \\(a\\) and \\(b\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(M\\) have normalized trace \\(\\tau\\). Choose a projection \\(p\\in M\\) with\n\\[\n\\tau(p)=\\frac12.\n\\]\nSet\n\\[\nN=pMp,\n\\qquad\n\\tau_N(a)=2\\tau(a).\n\\]\nThis corner is a type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) factor with separable predual: precomposition with the normal compression\n\\[\na\\longmapsto pap\n\\]\nembeds \\(N_*\\) isometrically into \\(M_*\\).</p><p>Since \\(p\\) and \\(1-p\\) have the same trace, there is a partial isometry \\(v\\in M\\) with\n\\[\nv^*v=p,\n\\qquad\nvv^*=1-p.\n\\]\nThe corresponding matrix units give a normal unital \\(*\\)-isomorphism\n\\[\n\\Phi:M_2(N)\\longrightarrow M,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Phi\n\\begin{pmatrix}\nb_{11}&amp;b_{12}\\\\\nb_{21}&amp;b_{22}\n\\end{pmatrix}\n=\nb_{11}+b_{12}v^*+vb_{21}+vb_{22}v^*.\n\\]</p><p>Proposition 4, applied to \\(N\\), and Proposition 5 provide self-adjoint \\(A,B\\in M_2(N)\\) with\n\\[\nW^*(A,B)=M_2(N).\n\\]\nDefine\n\\[\nx=\\Phi(A+iB)\\in M.\n\\]\nSince the real and imaginary parts of \\(x\\) are \\(\\Phi(A)\\) and \\(\\Phi(B)\\), respectively,\n\\[\nW^*(x)\n=\nW^*(\\Phi(A),\\Phi(B))\n=\n\\Phi(M_2(N))\n=\nM.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a bounded operator \\(x\\in M\\) such that \\( M=W^*(x). \\)", "statement": "<p>**General von Neumann algebras — unnumbered consequence in Section 6.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(M\\) is a von Neumann algebra with separable predual; no finiteness or factoriality assumption is imposed.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(W^*(x)\\) denotes unital von Neumann algebra generation.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a bounded operator \\(x\\in M\\) such that\n\\[\nM=W^*(x).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Willig’s direct-integral reduction [22, Corollary 2], together with the established type I and properly infinite cases, reduces the generator problem for von Neumann algebras with separable predual to the type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) factor case. Theorem 2 therefore gives single generation for every von Neumann algebra with separable predual.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\mathcal G(M)=\\mathcal G_{\\mathrm{sa}}(M)=0. \\) These are values of the defining infima; no generating set or projection partition attaining zero is asserted.", "statement": "<p>**Vanishing of the generator invariants — unnumbered consequence in Section 6.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(M\\) is a complex type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) factor with separable predual and normalized trace.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\mathcal G\\) and \\(\\mathcal G_{\\mathrm{sa}}\\) are the generator invariants of [4, Definition 2.7]. They are nonnegative infima over finite generating sets and finite projection partitions; \\(\\mathcal G_{\\mathrm{sa}}\\) restricts the generating sets to self-adjoint elements.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\mathcal G(M)=\\mathcal G_{\\mathrm{sa}}(M)=0.\n\\]\nThese are values of the defining infima; no generating set or projection partition attaining zero is asserted.</p>", "proof": "<p>By [4, Corollary 5.1], a positive \\(\\mathcal G\\)-value for one complex type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) factor with separable predual would force a nonsingly generated factor in the same class, contrary to the universal assertion of Theorem 2.</p><p>The cited generation convention need not include the unit. To pass to that convention, replace a generator \\(x\\) by\n\\[\ny=x+\\lambda1,\n\\qquad\n\\lambda&gt;\\|x\\|.\n\\]\nThe invertible \\(y\\) has support projection \\(1\\) in its possibly nonunital von Neumann closure; that closure then contains \\(x\\) and equals \\(M\\).</p><p>Nonnegativity gives\n\\[\n\\mathcal G(M)=0,\n\\]\nand [4, Theorem 5.5] gives\n\\[\n\\mathcal G_{\\mathrm{sa}}(M)=2\\mathcal G(M)=0.\n\\]\nThese values are the defining infima; no generating set or projection partition attaining zero is asserted.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The factor \\(L(\\mathbb F_n)\\) has two finite self-adjoint generating tuples on which both \\(\\delta\\) and \\(\\delta_0\\) take different values.", "statement": "<p>**Dependence of free entropy dimensions on generators — original Corollary 6.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(n\\geq3\\) is an integer.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(L(\\mathbb F_n)\\) has its canonical trace.</p><p class=\"li\">For a bounded finite self-adjoint tuple \\(Z\\), \\(\\delta(Z)\\) and \\(\\delta_0(Z)\\) denote Voiculescu’s microstates free entropy dimension and its modified version, respectively [21, Section 2.4].</p><p class=\"li\">A \\(W^*\\)-generating tuple generates the tracial von Neumann algebra as a unital von Neumann algebra.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The factor \\(L(\\mathbb F_n)\\) has two finite self-adjoint generating tuples on which both \\(\\delta\\) and \\(\\delta_0\\) take different values. In particular, neither dimension is determined by the generated tracial von Neumann algebra.</p>", "proof": "<p>For a bounded self-adjoint \\(d\\)-tuple \\(Z\\), one has\n\\[\n\\delta(Z)\\leq d,\n\\qquad\n\\delta_0(Z)\\leq d.\n\\]\nBoth dimensions equal \\(n\\) for a freely independent variance-one semicircular \\(n\\)-tuple: see [21, Section 2.4(a), (c), and (d)] for \\(\\delta\\), and [2, Section 5] for \\(\\delta_0\\).</p><p>The standard semicircular \\(n\\)-tuple generates \\(L(\\mathbb F_n)\\) as a von Neumann algebra. A self-adjoint pair supplied by Theorem 2 generates the same factor and has each dimension at most\n\\[\n2&lt;n.\n\\]\nThese two tuples give the assertion.</p><p>The paper separately records a stronger cited conclusion, not used in this proof: the isomorphism argument in *An isomorphism of the free group factors* [10, Corollary 7.1] gives that each of \\(\\delta,\\delta_0\\) and the nonmicrostates dimensions \\(\\delta^*,\\delta^\\star\\) attains every integer value \\(n\\geq2\\) on finite self-adjoint \\(W^*\\)-generating tuples of \\(L(\\mathbb F_2)\\). That conclusion uses the trace-preserving isomorphisms between free group factors established in the cited paper. The present corollary uses only Theorem 2 and the standard dimension bounds above.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "One may successively choose \\(t_j\\in\\mathcal U(\\mathbf Q)\\), \\(0\\leq j\\leq r\\), such that \\(t_j\\) is Haar and free from \\(D_j\\), where \\( D_{j+1}=C^*(D_j,t_j). \\) Every \\(D_j\\) is norm separable.", "statement": "<p>**Free Haar unitaries in an ultrapower — the construction and absorption results of Section 2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(P\\subset M\\) is an irreducible inclusion of type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) factors. Separability of \\(M\\) is not required.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a free ultrafilter \\(\\omega\\) on \\(\\mathbb N\\), and put\n\\[\n\\mathbf M=M^\\omega\n=\\ell^\\infty(\\mathbb N,M)/\\mathcal I_\\omega,\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal I_\\omega\n=\\{(b_n):\\lim_{n\\to\\omega}\\|b_n\\|_2=0\\},\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf Q=P^\\omega.\n\\]\nThe trace of the element represented by \\((b_n)\\) is \\(\\lim_{n\\to\\omega}\\tau(b_n)\\). Identify \\(M\\) with its constant copy in \\(\\mathbf M\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A unitary \\(t\\) is Haar if \\(\\tau(t^m)=0\\) for every nonzero integer \\(m\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Two unital subalgebras are free if every alternating product of centered elements from the two subalgebras has trace zero. A unitary \\(t\\) is free from \\(D\\) if \\(C^*(t)\\) and \\(D\\) are free.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(D_0\\subset\\mathbf M\\) is a norm-separable unital \\(C^*\\)-algebra, and \\(r\\geq0\\) is an integer.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>One may successively choose \\(t_j\\in\\mathcal U(\\mathbf Q)\\), \\(0\\leq j\\leq r\\), such that \\(t_j\\) is Haar and free from \\(D_j\\), where\n\\[\nD_{j+1}=C^*(D_j,t_j).\n\\]\nEvery \\(D_j\\) is norm separable.</p><p>For a Haar unitary \\(t\\) free from a unital \\(C^*\\)-algebra \\(D\\), the endpoint moment identity is\n\\[\n\\tau(d_0t^{n_1}d_1\\cdots t^{n_j}d_j)=0\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\nwhenever \\(j\\geq1\\), \\(n_1,\\ldots,n_j\\in\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0\\}\\), \\(d_0,\\ldots,d_j\\in D\\), and\n\\[\n\\tau(d_i)=0\\qquad(1\\leq i\\leq j-1).\n\\]\nThe endpoints \\(d_0,d_j\\) are arbitrary.</p><p>The following absorption statement also holds, without the ultrapower or irreducibility assumptions: in any finite tracial von Neumann algebra, if \\(t\\) is Haar and free from \\(D\\), and \\(a,b\\in\\mathcal U(D)\\), then \\(atb\\) and \\(t^*\\) are Haar and free from \\(D\\), and both satisfy the same endpoint moment identity.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Lemma 1.3 successively: choose \\(t_j\\in\\mathbf Q\\) Haar and free from \\(D_j\\), and set\n\\[\nD_{j+1}=C^*(D_j,t_j),\n\\qquad 0\\leq j\\leq r.\n\\]\nEach \\(D_{j+1}\\) remains norm separable: polynomials in a countable norm-dense subset of \\(D_j\\), the two elements \\(t_j,t_j^*\\), and rational complex coefficients form a countable dense set. Thus every application of the lemma has the required separability hypothesis, even though the previously chosen unitaries already belong to \\(\\mathbf Q\\).</p><p>Lemma 1.3 gives the endpoint moment identity, and Lemma 1.4 gives the absorption statement in the stated generality.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\mathbf Q'\\cap\\mathbf M=\\mathbb C1. \\)", "statement": "<p>**Ultrapower irreducibility — original Lemma 2.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(P\\subset M\\), \\(\\omega\\), \\(\\mathbf Q=P^\\omega\\), and \\(\\mathbf M=M^\\omega\\) are as in Proposition 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\mathbf Q'\\cap\\mathbf M=\\mathbb C1.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(b\\in\\mathbf Q'\\cap\\mathbf M\\) have a uniformly bounded representative \\((b_n)\\), and put\n\\[\n\\delta_n=\\sup_{w\\in\\mathcal U(P)}\n\\|wb_nw^*-b_n\\|_2.\n\\]\nThen \\(\\lim_{n\\to\\omega}\\delta_n=0\\). Otherwise there would be an \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\) and a set belonging to \\(\\omega\\) on which \\(\\delta_n&gt;\\varepsilon\\). Choosing on this set a unitary \\(w_n\\in P\\) with\n\\[\n\\|w_nb_nw_n^*-b_n\\|_2&gt;\\varepsilon,\n\\]\nand setting \\(w_n=1\\) elsewhere, would give a unitary in \\(\\mathbf Q\\) that does not commute with \\(b\\).</p><p>For each \\(n\\), let \\(K_n\\) be the closed convex hull, in \\(L^2(M,\\tau)\\), of\n\\[\n\\{wb_nw^*:w\\in\\mathcal U(P)\\}.\n\\]\nThe unique element \\(c_n\\in K_n\\) of smallest \\(2\\)-norm is fixed by conjugation by every unitary of \\(P\\): each such conjugation preserves \\(K_n\\) and its Hilbert space norm.</p><p>Moreover, \\(c_n\\in M\\) and \\(\\|c_n\\|\\leq\\|b_n\\|\\). Indeed, convex combinations from this orbit have this operator norm bound, and an operator-norm-bounded \\(2\\)-norm limit lies in the same bounded ball of \\(M\\). To see the latter fact, left multiplication by such a convergent sequence converges strongly on the dense subspace \\(M\\subset L^2(M)\\), since\n\\[\n\\|(x_r-x_s)a\\|_2\\leq\\|x_r-x_s\\|_2\\|a\\|\n\\qquad(a\\in M),\n\\]\nand hence converges strongly on all of \\(L^2(M)\\) by the uniform operator norm bound. The strong limit belongs to \\(M\\), and its value at \\(1\\) is the prescribed \\(2\\)-norm limit.</p><p>Thus\n\\[\nc_n\\in P'\\cap M=\\mathbb C1.\n\\]\nThe trace is constant on \\(K_n\\) and continuous in \\(2\\)-norm, so \\(c_n=\\tau(b_n)1\\). Every element of \\(K_n\\) is at \\(2\\)-distance at most \\(\\delta_n\\) from \\(b_n\\). Consequently,\n\\[\n\\|b_n-\\tau(b_n)1\\|_2\\leq\\delta_n\n\\longrightarrow_{n\\to\\omega}0.\n\\]\nThe bounded scalar sequence \\((\\tau(b_n))\\) has a scalar ultralimit, which represents \\(b\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a diffuse abelian von Neumann algebra \\(A\\subset\\mathbf Q\\) such that \\( \\tau(x_0a_1x_1\\cdots a_mx_m)=0 \\) whenever \\(m\\geq1\\), \\( x_0\\in X\\cup\\{1\\},\\qquad x_1,\\ldots,x_m\\in X, \\) and \\(a_1,\\ldots,a_m\\in A\\) …", "statement": "<p>**Popa’s independence theorem — original Theorem 2.2, used as an external input.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(P\\subset M\\) is an irreducible inclusion of type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) factors.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\mathbf Q=P^\\omega\\subset M^\\omega=\\mathbf M\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(X\\subset\\mathbf M\\) is a linear subspace that is separable in \\(2\\)-norm and satisfies \\(\\tau(x)=0\\) for every \\(x\\in X\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a diffuse abelian von Neumann algebra \\(A\\subset\\mathbf Q\\) such that\n\\[\n\\tau(x_0a_1x_1\\cdots a_mx_m)=0\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\nwhenever \\(m\\geq1\\),\n\\[\nx_0\\in X\\cup\\{1\\},\\qquad x_1,\\ldots,x_m\\in X,\n\\]\nand \\(a_1,\\ldots,a_m\\in A\\) have trace zero.</p>", "proof": "<p>This is the constant-inclusion case of [15, Theorem 0.1(a)]. The atomic-relative-commutant case needed here already appears in [14, Lemma 1.4 and Theorem 2.1].</p><p>The hypothesis in [15] is that no nonzero corner of the coordinate algebra \\(P\\) intertwines into \\(P'\\cap M\\) inside \\(M\\). It holds here because \\(P'\\cap M=\\mathbb C1\\): a nonzero corner of the diffuse factor \\(P\\) cannot embed normally into a finite matrix amplification of the scalars.</p><p>The conclusion of that theorem is independence relative to \\(\\mathbf Q'\\cap\\mathbf M\\). Lemma 1.1 identifies this commutant with the scalars, giving (2.1).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a Haar unitary \\(t\\in\\mathbf Q\\) free from \\(D\\).", "statement": "<p>**A free Haar unitary — original Lemma 2.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(P\\subset M\\), \\(\\mathbf Q\\), and \\(\\mathbf M\\) are as in Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(D\\subset\\mathbf M\\) is a norm-separable unital \\(C^*\\)-algebra.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a Haar unitary \\(t\\in\\mathbf Q\\) free from \\(D\\). In particular,\n\\[\n\\tau(d_0t^{n_1}d_1\\cdots t^{n_j}d_j)=0\n\\]\nfor every \\(j\\geq1\\), all nonzero integers \\(n_1,\\ldots,n_j\\), and all \\(d_0,\\ldots,d_j\\in D\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\tau(d_i)=0\\qquad(1\\leq i\\leq j-1).\n\\]\nThe two endpoints \\(d_0,d_j\\) are arbitrary.</p>", "proof": "<p>The subspace \\(X=D\\cap\\ker\\tau\\) is norm separable, hence \\(2\\)-norm separable. For example, applying\n\\[\nd\\longmapsto d-\\tau(d)1\n\\]\nto a countable norm-dense subset of \\(D\\) gives a countable norm-dense subset of \\(X\\). Apply Lemma 1.2 to obtain a diffuse abelian algebra \\(A\\subset\\mathbf Q\\).</p><p>First choose a Haar unitary in \\(A\\). Diffuseness gives arbitrarily small nonzero subprojections of every nonzero projection: repeatedly split and retain a piece of at most half the trace. Normality of the trace and maximality under inclusion then give subprojections of every prescribed smaller trace. We may therefore choose recursively partitions of \\(1\\) by projections\n\\[\n\\{p_{m,k}:0\\leq k&lt;2^m\\}\\subset A,\n\\qquad\n\\tau(p_{m,k})=2^{-m},\n\\]\nwith\n\\[\np_{m,k}=p_{m+1,2k}+p_{m+1,2k+1}.\n\\]\nThe self-adjoint elements\n\\[\nh_m=\\sum_{k=0}^{2^m-1}\\frac{k}{2^m}p_{m,k}\n\\]\nsatisfy\n\\[\n\\|h_{m+1}-h_m\\|\\leq2^{-(m+1)}\n\\]\nand converge in norm to an element \\(h\\in A\\). For every continuous function \\(f\\) on \\([0,1]\\),\n\\[\n\\tau(f(h))\n=\\lim_{m\\to\\infty}\n2^{-m}\\sum_{k=0}^{2^m-1}f(k2^{-m})\n=\\int_0^1f(s)\\,ds.\n\\]\nIt follows that \\(t=\\exp(2\\pi ih)\\) is Haar.</p><p>Next remove the centering requirement at the two endpoints, so that (2.2) can be applied to words with arbitrary outside coefficients. Expand each endpoint into its scalar and centered parts. A term with a centered last endpoint vanishes by (2.1), since every nonzero power of \\(t\\) belongs to \\(A\\) and is centered. A term with a centered first endpoint and scalar last endpoint vanishes after moving the first endpoint to the end by traciality.</p><p>It remains to consider the word with both endpoints omitted. When \\(j=1\\), this is the Haar property. When \\(j\\geq2\\), rotate the final power \\(t^{n_j}\\) next to the first power \\(t^{n_1}\\). If \\(n_j+n_1\\neq0\\), the resulting word is covered by (2.1). If \\(n_j+n_1=0\\), the two powers cancel and leave a word starting and ending with centered elements of \\(D\\). This word is again covered by (2.1), except when \\(j=2\\), in which case it is simply the centered element \\(d_1\\). This proves the endpoint identity (2.2).</p><p>Laurent polynomials in \\(t\\) are norm dense in \\(C^*(t)\\). As \\(t\\) is Haar, the centered Laurent polynomials are precisely the linear combinations of its nonzero powers. Expanding an alternating centered word, and using (2.2) followed by norm approximation, proves that \\(t\\) is free from \\(D\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Both \\(atb\\) and \\(t^*\\) are Haar unitaries free from \\(D\\).", "statement": "<p>**Absorption of base-algebra unitaries — original Lemma 2.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For this lemma, replace the ultrapower setting by an arbitrary finite tracial von Neumann algebra \\((B,\\tau)\\); no irreducible inclusion is assumed.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(D\\subset B\\) is a unital \\(C^*\\)-subalgebra.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(t\\in B\\) is a Haar unitary free from \\(D\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(a,b\\in\\mathcal U(D)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Both \\(atb\\) and \\(t^*\\) are Haar unitaries free from \\(D\\). Both satisfy (2.2) in place of \\(t\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For any Haar unitary free from \\(D\\), formula (2.2) follows immediately by expanding the endpoints into scalar and centered parts and applying freeness to each resulting alternating word. Replacing \\(t\\) by \\(t^*\\) merely negates the exponents, so concentrate on \\(v=atb\\).</p><p>Consider a word\n\\[\nd_0v^{n_1}d_1\\cdots v^{n_j}d_j\n\\]\nwith nonzero integer exponents and centered internal factors \\(d_1,\\ldots,d_{j-1}\\). Expand each power of \\(v\\) into individual letters \\(t\\) and \\(t^{-1}\\), with elements of \\(D\\) between them. Within one power of \\(v\\), all these letters have the same sign. A gap between opposite signs can therefore occur only between two successive powers of \\(v\\). Such a gap equals\n\\[\nbd_ib^*\n\\quad\\text{for a transition from }t\\text{ to }t^{-1},\n\\qquad\na^*d_ia\n\\quad\\text{for a transition from }t^{-1}\\text{ to }t.\n\\]\nIt is centered by traciality.</p><p>Every other gap joins equal signs. Expand each of these latter gaps into its scalar and centered parts. In a resulting term, removing a scalar gap only joins letters of equal sign. Thus each block joined across scalar gaps is a nonzero power of \\(t\\), and consecutive blocks are separated by centered elements of \\(D\\). The endpoint factors remain arbitrary elements of \\(D\\). Formula (2.2) shows that every resulting term has trace zero.</p><p>This proves (2.2) for \\(v\\). Taking \\(j=1\\) and \\(d_0=d_1=1\\) proves that \\(v\\) is Haar. The general formula and norm approximation by Laurent polynomials then prove its freeness from \\(D\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are \\(\\widetilde u\\in\\mathcal U(M)\\) and \\( \\widetilde t_0,\\ldots,\\widetilde t_\\ell\\in\\mathcal U(P) \\) such that, with \\( \\widetilde W =\\widetilde t_0\\prod_{k=1}^{\\ell} (\\widetilde u\\,\\widetilde t_k), \\) one has …", "statement": "<p>**A perturbation with uniform correlation — original Proposition 3.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(P\\subset M\\) is an irreducible inclusion of type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) factors. Separability is not assumed.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(u,z\\in\\mathcal U(M)\\), \\(\\xi\\in M\\), and \\(0&lt;\\alpha&lt;1/2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\ell\\geq1\\) is an integer.</p><p class=\"li\">All products indexed by \\(k\\) are taken in increasing order from left to right.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are \\(\\widetilde u\\in\\mathcal U(M)\\) and\n\\[\n\\widetilde t_0,\\ldots,\\widetilde t_\\ell\\in\\mathcal U(P)\n\\]\nsuch that, with\n\\[\n\\widetilde W\n=\\widetilde t_0\\prod_{k=1}^{\\ell}\n(\\widetilde u\\,\\widetilde t_k),\n\\]\none has\n\\[\n\\|\\widetilde u-u\\|_2\n\\leq\\frac{\\alpha}{\\sqrt{2\\ell}}+\\frac1\\ell,\n\\qquad\n\\left|\n\\tau(\\xi^*\\widetilde W)\n-\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\tau(\\xi^*z)\n\\right|\n\\leq\n\\|\\xi\\|_2\\frac{4\\alpha^2}{1-2\\alpha}\n+\\frac1\\ell.\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\nThe unitaries may depend on \\(\\ell\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(\\ell\\) and work first in \\(\\mathbf M=M^\\omega\\), with \\(\\mathbf Q=P^\\omega\\). Use \\(\\tau\\) also for the ultrapower trace.</p><p>**Free variables and prefixes.** Set\n\\[\nD_0=C^*(1,u,z,\\xi).\n\\]\nBy Proposition 1, choose successively \\(t_j\\in\\mathcal U(\\mathbf Q)\\) Haar and free from \\(D_j\\), and put\n\\[\nD_{j+1}=C^*(D_j,t_j),\n\\qquad 0\\leq j\\leq\\ell.\n\\]\nEach of these \\(C^*\\)-algebras is norm separable. Define\n\\[\ns_1=t_0,\n\\qquad\ns_{j+1}=ut_j\\quad(1\\leq j\\leq\\ell),\n\\qquad\ng_k=s_1\\cdots s_k\\quad(1\\leq k\\leq\\ell),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nW=g_\\ell s_{\\ell+1}\n=t_0\\prod_{k=1}^{\\ell}(ut_k).\n\\]\nThus \\(g_k\\) is the prefix immediately preceding the \\(k\\)-th occurrence of \\(u\\) in \\(W\\).</p><p>Since\n\\[\nD_j=C^*(D_0,s_1,\\ldots,s_j),\n\\]\nthe absorption statement in Proposition 1 shows that \\(s_{j+1}\\) is Haar and free from \\(D_j\\). In particular, \\(s_1,\\ldots,s_\\ell\\) are free Haar unitaries. Every nonidentity reduced word in them has trace zero, so the group they generate can be identified with the free group \\(G\\) on these generators.</p><p>Since\n\\[\nW=(g_\\ell u)t_\\ell,\n\\qquad\ng_\\ell u\\in\\mathcal U(D_\\ell),\n\\]\nthe same absorption statement shows that \\(W\\) is Haar and free from \\(D_\\ell\\). Applying it to \\(W^*\\) and \\(z\\in\\mathcal U(D_\\ell)\\) also shows that\n\\[\ny=zW^*\n\\]\nis Haar and free from \\(D_\\ell\\).</p><p>For \\(p\\in G\\), set\n\\[\nY_p=pyp^{-1}.\n\\]\nThese form a free family of Haar unitaries. Indeed, if \\(a_1,\\ldots,a_r\\) are nonzero integers and successive labels \\(p_1,\\ldots,p_r\\) are distinct, then\n\\[\nY_{p_1}^{a_1}\\cdots Y_{p_r}^{a_r}\n=\np_1y^{a_1}(p_1^{-1}p_2)y^{a_2}\n\\cdots(p_{r-1}^{-1}p_r)y^{a_r}p_r^{-1}.\n\\]\nEach internal ratio is a nonidentity element of \\(G\\), hence is centered in \\(D_\\ell\\). Equation (2.2), supplied by Proposition 1, gives zero trace. Applying this to the reduction of \\(v^*w\\) shows that distinct reduced words in the \\(Y_p\\) are orthonormal in \\(L^2(\\mathbf M,\\tau)\\), including the empty word.</p><p>A left perturbation of \\(u\\) at its \\(k\\)-th occurrence in \\(W\\) is conjugated by \\(g_k\\) when moved to the front of the word. Therefore use the conjugates \\(g_j^{-1}yg_j\\): conjugating the \\(j\\)-th one by \\(g_j\\) returns \\(y\\). Put\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Im}(b)=\\frac{b-b^*}{2i}\n\\]\nand define\n\\[\nh=\\frac{\\alpha}{\\ell}\n\\sum_{j=1}^{\\ell}\\operatorname{Im}(g_j^{-1}yg_j),\n\\qquad\nu'=e^{ih}u,\n\\qquad\nW'=t_0\\prod_{k=1}^{\\ell}(u't_k).\n\\]\nThe \\(g_j^{-1}\\) are distinct in \\(G\\), so the summands defining \\(h\\) are orthogonal centered imaginary parts of free Haar unitaries. Each has squared \\(2\\)-norm \\(1/2\\). Consequently,\n\\[\nh=h^*,\n\\qquad\n\\|h\\|\\leq\\alpha,\n\\qquad\n\\|h\\|_2=\\frac{\\alpha}{\\sqrt{2\\ell}}.\n\\]</p><p>Write\n\\[\nH_k=g_khg_k^{-1},\n\\qquad\np_{kj}=g_kg_j^{-1},\n\\qquad\nc=\\frac{\\alpha}{2\\ell}.\n\\]\nCancelling consecutive prefixes gives\n\\[\nW'=\\left(\\prod_{k=1}^{\\ell}e^{iH_k}\\right)W,\n\\qquad\niH_k=c\\sum_{j=1}^{\\ell}\n\\left(Y_{p_{kj}}-Y_{p_{kj}}^{-1}\\right).\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\nFor the first identity, use\n\\[\ne^{iH_k}=g_ke^{ih}g_k^{-1},\n\\qquad\ng_k^{-1}g_{k+1}=ut_k\\quad(1\\leq k&lt;\\ell);\n\\]\nthe product order is therefore essential.</p><p>Let\n\\[\nS=\\{p_{kj}:1\\leq k,j\\leq\\ell\\},\n\\]\nand let \\(n_p\\) count the ordered pairs \\((k,j)\\) with \\(p_{kj}=p\\). For the identity \\(e\\) of \\(G\\), \\(n_e=\\ell\\). If \\(k\\neq j\\), the word\n\\[\np_{kj}\n=s_1\\cdots s_ks_j^{-1}\\cdots s_1^{-1}\n\\]\nis reduced, since its joining letters have different indices. Its initial positive segment and its final negative segment determine \\(k\\) and \\(j\\). All off-diagonal pairs therefore give distinct nonidentity words, and\n\\[\n\\sum_{p\\in S}n_p^2\n=\\ell^2+\\ell(\\ell-1)\n=2\\ell^2-\\ell.\n\\tag{3.3}\n\\]</p><p>**The first-order correlation.** Put\n\\[\nC_1=\\sum_{k=1}^{\\ell}iH_k.\n\\]\nEach diagonal term \\(cY_{p_{kk}}W\\) equals \\(cz\\), because \\(p_{kk}=e\\) and \\(Y_eW=z\\). All other linear terms vanish when paired with \\(\\xi\\). Equation (2.2) for \\(W\\) and \\(D_\\ell\\) gives\n\\[\n\\tau(\\xi^*W)=0.\n\\]\nFor \\(p\\neq e\\), the element \\(p^{-1}\\) is centered in \\(D_\\ell\\), so\n\\[\n\\tau(\\xi^*Y_pW)\n=\\tau(\\xi^*pzW^*p^{-1}W)=0.\n\\]\nFor every \\(p\\in G\\), one also has\n\\[\n\\tau(\\xi^*Y_p^{-1}W)\n=\\tau(\\xi^*pWz^*p^{-1}W)=0.\n\\]\nIndeed, split the internal factor \\(z^*p^{-1}\\in D_\\ell\\) into its centered and scalar parts. The first contribution vanishes by (2.2); the second is a scalar multiple of \\(\\tau(\\xi^*pW^2)\\), which vanishes by the same formula. Therefore\n\\[\n\\tau\\bigl(\\xi^*(1+C_1)W\\bigr)\n=cn_e\\tau(\\xi^*z)\n=\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\tau(\\xi^*z).\n\\]</p><p>**The uniform remainder estimate.** Group the ordered product of exponentials by its original degree:\n\\[\nC_d=\n\\sum_{\\substack{m_1+\\cdots+m_\\ell=d\\\\m_k\\geq0}}\n\\frac{(iH_1)^{m_1}\\cdots(iH_\\ell)^{m_\\ell}}\n{m_1!\\cdots m_\\ell!}.\n\\]\nThe term \\(C_0\\) is \\(1\\), and the degree-one term is the \\(C_1\\) above. For fixed \\(\\ell\\), the expansion is absolutely convergent in operator norm, because\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sum_{d\\geq0}\\|C_d\\|\n&amp;\\leq\n\\prod_{k=1}^{\\ell}\n\\sum_{m\\geq0}\\frac{\\|H_k\\|^m}{m!}\\\\\n&amp;=\n\\exp\\left(\\sum_{k=1}^{\\ell}\\|H_k\\|\\right)\n\\leq e^{\\ell\\alpha}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe Cauchy product may thus be grouped in this way, and its sum is \\(\\prod_k e^{iH_k}\\). The required uniform bound is\n\\[\n\\left\\|\n\\prod_{k=1}^{\\ell}e^{iH_k}-1-C_1\n\\right\\|_2\n\\leq\\frac{4\\alpha^2}{1-2\\alpha}.\n\\tag{3.4}\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(F_S\\) be the formal free group with generators \\(T_p\\), \\(p\\in S\\). In particular, \\(T_e\\) is a generator, not the identity of \\(F_S\\). Let\n\\[\n\\Phi:\\mathbb C[F_S]\\longrightarrow\\mathbf M,\n\\qquad\n\\Phi(T_p)=Y_p.\n\\]\nBy the orthonormality proved above, every finite group-ring sum \\(f=\\sum_{w\\in F_S}f(w)w\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\|\\Phi(f)\\|_2^2\n=\\sum_{w\\in F_S}|f(w)|^2.\n\\tag{3.5}\n\\]</p><p>Define\n\\[\nB_k=c\\sum_{j=1}^{\\ell}\n\\left(T_{p_{kj}}-T_{p_{kj}}^{-1}\\right),\n\\qquad\nA_k=c\\sum_{j=1}^{\\ell}\n\\left(T_{p_{kj}}+T_{p_{kj}}^{-1}\\right),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nA=\\sum_{k=1}^{\\ell}A_k\n=c\\sum_{p\\in S}n_p(T_p+T_p^{-1}).\n\\]\nFor a group-ring element \\(f\\), write \\(|f|\\) for its coefficientwise absolute value, and compare nonnegative coefficient functions coefficientwise. Group multiplication gives\n\\[\n|(fg)(w)|\n=\n\\left|\\sum_{ab=w}f(a)g(b)\\right|\n\\leq\n\\sum_{ab=w}|f(a)||g(b)|\n=(|f||g|)(w).\n\\]\nThis comparison already includes all free reductions and all contributions collected at the same resulting word.</p><p>Thus the formal element\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_d\n=\n\\sum_{\\substack{m_1+\\cdots+m_\\ell=d\\\\m_k\\geq0}}\n\\frac{B_1^{m_1}\\cdots B_\\ell^{m_\\ell}}\n{m_1!\\cdots m_\\ell!},\n\\qquad\n\\Phi(\\mathcal C_d)=C_d,\n\\]\nsatisfies\n\\[\n|\\mathcal C_d|\n\\leq\n\\sum_{\\substack{m_1+\\cdots+m_\\ell=d\\\\m_k\\geq0}}\n\\frac{A_1^{m_1}\\cdots A_\\ell^{m_\\ell}}\n{m_1!\\cdots m_\\ell!}\n\\leq A^d.\n\\tag{3.6}\n\\]\nFor the last inequality, each tuple \\((m_1,\\ldots,m_\\ell)\\) corresponds to one nondecreasing sequence of row indices in the distributive expansion of \\((A_1+\\cdots+A_\\ell)^d\\). Its factorial weight is at most one, and every other summand has nonnegative coefficients. No commutation of the \\(A_k\\) is used. The index \\(d\\) records the degree before reduction; resulting words may have shorter length, including zero.</p><p>Let \\(\\lambda\\) now denote the left regular representation of \\(F_S\\), and let \\(\\delta_{\\mathrm{id}}\\) be the unit vector at its identity. By (3.5) and (3.6),\n\\[\n\\|C_d\\|_2\n\\leq\\|A^d\\|_{\\ell^2(F_S)}\n=\\|\\lambda(A)^d\\delta_{\\mathrm{id}}\\|_{\\ell^2(F_S)}\n\\leq\\|\\lambda(A)\\|^d.\n\\]\nThis uses only the coefficient \\(2\\)-norm identity (3.5), with no comparison of operator norms between the two representations. Lemma 2.1 and (3.3) give\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|\\lambda(A)\\|\n&amp;\\leq\n2\\left(2c^2\\sum_{p\\in S}n_p^2\\right)^{1/2}\\\\\n&amp;=\n2\\alpha\\left(1-\\frac1{2\\ell}\\right)^{1/2}\n\\leq2\\alpha&lt;1.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\|C_d\\|_2\\leq(2\\alpha)^d.\n\\]\nThe operator-norm convergence established above implies convergence of the same partial sums in \\(2\\)-norm. Taking their tails and using the triangle inequality gives\n\\[\n\\left\\|\n\\prod_{k=1}^{\\ell}e^{iH_k}-1-C_1\n\\right\\|_2\n\\leq\n\\sum_{d\\geq2}(2\\alpha)^d\n=\\frac{4\\alpha^2}{1-2\\alpha}.\n\\]\nIn particular, orthogonality between different degrees is not required. This proves (3.4).</p><p>Combining the first-order correlation with (3.2), (3.4), and Cauchy–Schwarz gives\n\\[\n\\left|\n\\tau(\\xi^*W')\n-\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\tau(\\xi^*z)\n\\right|\n\\leq\n\\|\\xi\\|_2\\frac{4\\alpha^2}{1-2\\alpha}.\n\\tag{3.7}\n\\]</p><p>**Lifting for the fixed word length.** Each \\(t_j\\) has a representative consisting of unitaries in \\(P\\). To see this, start with a bounded representative \\((b_n)\\) of any unitary in \\(P^\\omega\\). The polar part of \\(b_n\\) extends to a unitary \\(w_n\\in P\\), since its initial and final complementary projections have equal trace in the finite factor \\(P\\). Then \\(b_n=w_n|b_n|\\), and functional calculus gives\n\\[\n\\|b_n-w_n\\|_2\n=\\||b_n|-1\\|_2\n\\leq\\|b_n^*b_n-1\\|_2\n\\longrightarrow_{n\\to\\omega}0.\n\\]\nThe inequality follows from\n\\[\n|\\sqrt t-1|\\leq|t-1|\\qquad(t\\geq0).\n\\]</p><p>Choose unitary representatives \\(t_{j,n}\\in P\\) for the finitely many \\(t_j\\). Using the constant representatives of \\(u,z\\), form\n\\[\ng_{k,n},\\ W_n,\\ y_n,\\ h_n,\\ u_n',\\ W_n'\n\\]\nby exactly the formulas above. For this fixed \\(\\ell\\), these are bounded sequences, with\n\\[\nh_n=h_n^*,\n\\qquad\n\\|h_n\\|\\leq\\alpha.\n\\]\nBounded algebraic operations and continuous functional calculus in the quotient show that they represent the corresponding ultrapower elements. In particular,\n\\[\n\\lim_{n\\to\\omega}\\|h_n\\|_2\n=\\frac{\\alpha}{\\sqrt{2\\ell}},\n\\qquad\n\\lim_{n\\to\\omega}\\tau(\\xi^*W_n')\n=\\tau(\\xi^*W').\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\n\\|u_n'-u\\|_2\\leq\\|h_n\\|_2,\n\\]\nby \\(|e^{it}-1|\\leq|t|\\) and functional calculus.</p><p>Choose one index \\(n\\) for which\n\\[\n\\|h_n\\|_2\n&lt;\\frac{\\alpha}{\\sqrt{2\\ell}}+\\frac1\\ell,\n\\qquad\n|\\tau(\\xi^*W_n')-\\tau(\\xi^*W')|&lt;\\frac1\\ell.\n\\]\nBoth conditions hold on sets belonging to \\(\\omega\\), so their intersection is nonempty. Set\n\\[\n\\widetilde u=u_n',\n\\qquad\n\\widetilde t_j=t_{j,n}.\n\\]\nEquation (3.7) proves (3.1). This choice is made separately for each \\(\\ell\\), as the proposition permits.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\left\\|\\sum_{\\gamma\\in L}b_\\gamma\\lambda(\\gamma)\\right\\| \\leq 2\\left(\\sum_{\\gamma\\in L}|b_\\gamma|^2\\right)^{1/2}. \\)", "statement": "<p>**The length-one case of Haagerup’s inequality — original Lemma 3.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This lemma concerns a free group and does not require the inclusion in Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(F\\) is a free group with a specified free basis.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(L\\) is the set of basis elements and their inverses.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\lambda\\) is the left regular representation on \\(\\ell^2(F)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\((b_\\gamma)_{\\gamma\\in L}\\) is finitely supported.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\left\\|\\sum_{\\gamma\\in L}b_\\gamma\\lambda(\\gamma)\\right\\|\n\\leq\n2\\left(\\sum_{\\gamma\\in L}|b_\\gamma|^2\\right)^{1/2}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For a letter \\(a\\), let \\(B_a\\) be the orthogonal projection onto the closed span of reduced words beginning with \\(a\\). Split\n\\[\n\\lambda(\\gamma)\n=\\lambda(\\gamma)(1-B_{\\gamma^{-1}})\n+\\lambda(\\gamma)B_{\\gamma^{-1}}.\n\\]\nThe first summand has range in the range of \\(B_\\gamma\\). These ranges are mutually orthogonal, so for \\(\\eta\\in\\ell^2(F)\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left\\|\n\\sum_\\gamma b_\\gamma\n\\lambda(\\gamma)(1-B_{\\gamma^{-1}})\\eta\n\\right\\|^2\n&amp;=\n\\sum_\\gamma |b_\\gamma|^2\n\\|(1-B_{\\gamma^{-1}})\\eta\\|^2\\\\\n&amp;\\leq\n\\left(\\sum_\\gamma|b_\\gamma|^2\\right)\\|\\eta\\|^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nFor the second summands, the triangle inequality and Cauchy–Schwarz give\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left\\|\n\\sum_\\gamma b_\\gamma\\lambda(\\gamma)B_{\\gamma^{-1}}\\eta\n\\right\\|\n&amp;\\leq\n\\sum_\\gamma|b_\\gamma|\\|B_{\\gamma^{-1}}\\eta\\|\\\\\n&amp;\\leq\n\\left(\\sum_\\gamma|b_\\gamma|^2\\right)^{1/2}\\|\\eta\\|,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nbecause the projections \\(B_{\\gamma^{-1}}\\) are mutually orthogonal. Adding the two estimates proves the lemma.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Each \\(f_\\xi\\) is bounded and lower semicontinuous, and each \\(d_\\xi\\) is upper semicontinuous.", "statement": "<p>**Continuity points yield relative generators — Section 4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(P\\subset M\\) is an irreducible inclusion of type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) factors, and \\(M\\) has separable predual.</p><p class=\"li\">The metric on \\(\\mathcal U(M)\\) is\n\\[\nd(u,v)=\\|u-v\\|_2.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(u\\in\\mathcal U(M)\\), put\n\\[\nN_u=W^*(P,u),\n\\]\nand let\n\\[\ne_u:L^2(M,\\tau)\\longrightarrow L^2(N_u,\\tau)\n\\]\nbe the orthogonal projection.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the inner product\n\\[\n\\langle a,b\\rangle=\\tau(b^*a),\n\\]\nlinear in the first variable.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(\\xi\\in L^2(M,\\tau)\\), define\n\\[\nf_\\xi(u)=\\|e_u\\xi\\|_2,\n\\qquad\nd_\\xi(u)=\\operatorname{dist}\\bigl(\\xi,L^2(N_u,\\tau)\\bigr).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Each \\(f_\\xi\\) is bounded and lower semicontinuous, and each \\(d_\\xi\\) is upper semicontinuous. There is a dense \\(G_\\delta\\) subset \\(\\mathcal C\\subset\\mathcal U(M)\\) such that every \\(f_\\xi\\), \\(\\xi\\in L^2(M,\\tau)\\), is continuous at every point of \\(\\mathcal C\\), and\n\\[\nW^*(P,u)=M\n\\qquad(u\\in\\mathcal C).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The semicontinuity assertions and the dense \\(G_\\delta\\) set \\(\\mathcal C\\) are supplied by Lemma 3.2. It remains to prove generation at every \\(u\\in\\mathcal C\\).</p><p>Take \\(u\\in\\mathcal C\\), and suppose \\(N_u\\neq M\\). There is a unitary\n\\[\nz\\in M\\setminus N_u,\n\\]\nsince the unitaries linearly span \\(M\\). Let\n\\[\nE_{N_u}:M\\longrightarrow N_u\n\\]\nbe the trace-preserving conditional expectation, and put\n\\[\n\\xi=z-E_{N_u}(z)\\in M,\n\\qquad\ns=\\|\\xi\\|_2&gt;0.\n\\]\nThe conditional expectation is the restriction to \\(M\\) of \\(e_u\\). Therefore\n\\[\ne_u\\xi=0,\n\\qquad\nf_\\xi(u)=0,\n\\qquad\n\\tau(\\xi^*z)=s^2.\n\\]</p><p>Fix\n\\[\n0&lt;\\alpha&lt;\\min\\{1/4,s/32\\}.\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\nFor every integer \\(\\ell\\geq1\\), Proposition 2 provides a unitary \\(u_\\ell\\in M\\) and unitaries\n\\[\nt_{0,\\ell},\\ldots,t_{\\ell,\\ell}\\in P\n\\]\nsuch that, with\n\\[\nW_\\ell\n=t_{0,\\ell}\\prod_{k=1}^{\\ell}(u_\\ell t_{k,\\ell})\n\\in\\mathcal U(N_{u_\\ell}),\n\\]\none has\n\\[\n\\|u_\\ell-u\\|_2\n\\leq\\frac{\\alpha}{\\sqrt{2\\ell}}+\\frac1\\ell\n\\tag{4.3}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\left|\n\\tau(\\xi^*W_\\ell)-\\frac{\\alpha s^2}{2}\n\\right|\n\\leq\ns\\frac{4\\alpha^2}{1-2\\alpha}+\\frac1\\ell.\n\\tag{4.4}\n\\]\nThe fixed choice (4.2) gives\n\\[\ns\\frac{4\\alpha^2}{1-2\\alpha}\n&lt;8s\\alpha^2\n&lt;\\frac{\\alpha s^2}{4}.\n\\]\nIt follows that\n\\[\n|\\tau(\\xi^*W_\\ell)|\n\\geq\n\\frac{\\alpha s^2}{4}-\\frac1\\ell\n\\geq\n\\frac{\\alpha s^2}{8}\n\\]\nfor all sufficiently large \\(\\ell\\).</p><p>On the other hand, \\(\\|W_\\ell\\|_2=1\\) and \\(W_\\ell\\in L^2(N_{u_\\ell},\\tau)\\), so\n\\[\n|\\tau(\\xi^*W_\\ell)|\n=\n|\\langle W_\\ell,e_{u_\\ell}\\xi\\rangle|\n\\leq f_\\xi(u_\\ell).\n\\]\nBy (4.3), \\(u_\\ell\\to u\\) in \\(2\\)-norm. Continuity of \\(f_\\xi\\) at \\(u\\) gives\n\\[\nf_\\xi(u_\\ell)\\longrightarrow f_\\xi(u)=0,\n\\]\na contradiction. Thus \\(M=W^*(P,u)\\) for every \\(u\\in\\mathcal C\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\(L^2(M,\\tau)\\) is separable, and \\(\\mathcal U(M)\\) is complete for \\(d\\).", "statement": "<p>**Separability and completeness — original Lemma 4.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(M\\) is a type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) factor with separable predual and normalized trace \\(\\tau\\). The subfactor \\(P\\) is not needed for this lemma.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(d(u,v)=\\|u-v\\|_2\\) on \\(\\mathcal U(M)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\(L^2(M,\\tau)\\) is separable, and \\(\\mathcal U(M)\\) is complete for \\(d\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The unit ball of \\(M\\) is compact and metrizable in the weak-star topology, so it has a countable weak-star dense subset \\(S\\). For each \\(\\xi\\in L^2(M,\\tau)\\), the functional\n\\[\na\\longmapsto\\langle a,\\xi\\rangle\n\\]\non \\(M\\) is normal. Indeed, normal functionals form a norm-closed subspace of \\(M^*\\), and approximation of \\(\\xi\\) by elements of \\(M\\) in \\(L^2\\) gives norm approximation of this functional by normal functionals, since\n\\[\n\\sup_{\\|a\\|\\leq1}\n|\\langle a,\\xi-\\eta\\rangle|\n\\leq\\|\\xi-\\eta\\|_2.\n\\]\nIf \\(\\xi\\) is orthogonal to \\(S\\), normality implies that it is orthogonal to the unit ball, hence to \\(M\\). Thus \\(S\\) has dense linear span in \\(L^2(M,\\tau)\\), proving separability.</p><p>Let \\((u_n)\\) be a Cauchy sequence in \\(\\mathcal U(M)\\) for \\(d\\). The adjoints are also Cauchy because\n\\[\n\\|u_n^*-u_m^*\\|_2=\\|u_n-u_m\\|_2.\n\\]\nFor \\(a\\in M\\),\n\\[\n\\|(u_n-u_m)a\\|_2\n\\leq\\|a\\|\\|u_n-u_m\\|_2.\n\\]\nThe left multiplication operators associated with \\(u_n\\) therefore converge strongly on the dense subspace \\(M\\subset L^2(M,\\tau)\\). Their uniform operator norm bound extends this convergence to the whole Hilbert space. The same holds for the left multipliers associated with \\(u_n^*\\).</p><p>Strong closedness of the left regular representation of \\(M\\) gives limits \\(u,v\\in M\\), and passage to inner products gives \\(v=u^*\\). Multiplication is jointly strongly continuous on bounded sets, so\n\\[\nuv=vu=1.\n\\]\nFinally, convergence on the vector \\(1\\) gives\n\\[\n\\|u_n-u\\|_2\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Each \\(f_\\xi\\) is bounded and lower semicontinuous on \\((\\mathcal U(M),d)\\).", "statement": "<p>**Simultaneous continuity — original Lemma 4.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the full setting of Proposition 3: \\(P\\subset M\\) is irreducible, \\(M\\) has separable predual, \\(N_u=W^*(P,u)\\), \\(e_u\\) is the \\(L^2\\)-projection onto \\(L^2(N_u,\\tau)\\), and \\(f_\\xi(u)=\\|e_u\\xi\\|_2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Each \\(f_\\xi\\) is bounded and lower semicontinuous on \\((\\mathcal U(M),d)\\). There is a dense \\(G_\\delta\\) subset \\(\\mathcal C\\subset\\mathcal U(M)\\) such that every \\(f_\\xi\\), \\(\\xi\\in L^2(M,\\tau)\\), is continuous at every point of \\(\\mathcal C\\).</p><p>In the proof, the distance function\n\\[\nd_\\xi(u)=\\operatorname{dist}\\bigl(\\xi,L^2(N_u,\\tau)\\bigr)\n\\]\nis upper semicontinuous.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(\\mathcal P_P\\) denote the family of finite \\(*\\)-polynomials in a unitary variable with coefficients in \\(P\\). For each fixed \\(u\\), the values\n\\[\nb(u),\\qquad b\\in\\mathcal P_P,\n\\]\nare dense in \\(L^2(N_u,\\tau)\\). To see this, take the operator norm closure of the polynomial algebra. Its von Neumann closure is \\(N_u\\), and Kaplansky density approximates each element of \\(N_u\\) strongly by bounded elements of this \\(C^*\\)-algebra. Evaluation on \\(1\\in L^2(M,\\tau)\\) gives \\(2\\)-norm approximation, which can then be followed by operator norm approximation by polynomials. The density of \\(N_u\\) in \\(L^2(N_u,\\tau)\\) finishes the assertion.</p><p>For fixed \\(b\\in\\mathcal P_P\\), the map\n\\[\nu\\longmapsto b(u)\n\\]\nis continuous in \\(2\\)-norm. This follows by telescoping products, using the fixed operator norm bounds of the coefficients, the fact that unitaries have norm one, and\n\\[\n\\|u^*-v^*\\|_2=\\|u-v\\|_2.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\nd_\\xi(u)\n=\n\\inf_{b\\in\\mathcal P_P}\\|\\xi-b(u)\\|_2\n\\]\nis upper semicontinuous: an infimum of any family of continuous real functions is upper semicontinuous. Orthogonality gives\n\\[\nf_\\xi(u)^2=\\|\\xi\\|_2^2-d_\\xi(u)^2,\n\\]\nso \\(f_\\xi\\) is lower semicontinuous and\n\\[\n0\\leq f_\\xi(u)\\leq\\|\\xi\\|_2.\n\\]</p><p>For completeness, a bounded lower semicontinuous real function \\(f\\) on a complete metric space is continuous on a dense \\(G_\\delta\\) set. For \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), let \\(G_\\varepsilon\\) be the union of all open sets on which the oscillation of \\(f\\) is less than \\(\\varepsilon\\). This union is dense. Indeed, in any nonempty open set \\(O\\), write\n\\[\ns=\\sup_O f\n\\]\nand choose \\(x\\in O\\) with \\(f(x)&gt;s-\\varepsilon/3\\). Lower semicontinuity gives an open neighborhood \\(V\\subset O\\) of \\(x\\) on which\n\\[\nf&gt;f(x)-\\varepsilon/3.\n\\]\nThe oscillation on \\(V\\) is less than \\(\\varepsilon\\). Baire’s theorem shows that\n\\[\n\\bigcap_{n\\geq1}G_{1/n}\n\\]\nis dense, and \\(f\\) is continuous at each of its points.</p><p>By Lemma 3.1, this applies on \\((\\mathcal U(M),d)\\). Apply it to \\(f_\\eta\\) for \\(\\eta\\) in a countable dense subset of \\(L^2(M,\\tau)\\), and intersect the resulting dense \\(G_\\delta\\) sets. Their intersection \\(\\mathcal C\\) is again a dense \\(G_\\delta\\) set.</p><p>For arbitrary \\(\\xi,\\eta\\in L^2(M,\\tau)\\), contractivity of \\(e_u\\) gives\n\\[\n|f_\\xi(u)-f_\\eta(u)|\n\\leq\\|\\xi-\\eta\\|_2\n\\qquad(u\\in\\mathcal U(M)).\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\nThus, for \\(u\\in\\mathcal C\\) and \\(v\\in\\mathcal U(M)\\),\n\\[\n|f_\\xi(v)-f_\\xi(u)|\n\\leq\n2\\|\\xi-\\eta\\|_2\n+|f_\\eta(v)-f_\\eta(u)|.\n\\]\nApproximating \\(\\xi\\) by an \\(\\eta\\) from the countable dense subset, and then letting \\(v\\to u\\), proves continuity of every \\(f_\\xi\\) at \\(u\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are four self-adjoint elements \\(a_1,a_2,a_3,a_4\\in M\\) such that \\( M=W^*(a_1,a_2,a_3,a_4). \\)", "statement": "<p>**Four self-adjoint generators — original Proposition 5.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(M\\) is a type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) factor with separable predual.</p><p class=\"li\">Generation is unital von Neumann algebra generation.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are four self-adjoint elements \\(a_1,a_2,a_3,a_4\\in M\\) such that\n\\[\nM=W^*(a_1,a_2,a_3,a_4).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>By Lemma 4.1, applied with \\(N=M\\), the factor \\(M\\) contains a hyperfinite type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) subfactor \\(P\\) with\n\\[\nP'\\cap M=\\mathbb C1.\n\\]\nThe trace-preserving conditional expectation onto \\(P\\) embeds \\(P_*\\) isometrically into \\(M_*\\) by precomposition, so \\(P\\) has separable predual.</p><p>Lemma 4.2 gives self-adjoint \\(a,b\\in P\\) with\n\\[\nP=W^*(a,b).\n\\]\nProposition 3 gives a unitary \\(u\\in\\mathcal U(M)\\) with\n\\[\nM=W^*(P,u).\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\nM=W^*(a,b,\\operatorname{Re}u,\\operatorname{Im}u).\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a hyperfinite type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) subfactor \\(R\\subset N\\) such that \\( R'\\cap M=\\mathbb C1. \\)", "statement": "<p>**Popa’s irreducible hyperfinite embedding theorem — original Theorem 5.1, used as an external input.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For this auxiliary input, \\(N\\subset M\\) is an irreducible inclusion of type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) factors with separable preduals.</p><p class=\"li\">Irreducibility means \\(N'\\cap M=\\mathbb C1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a hyperfinite type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) subfactor \\(R\\subset N\\) such that\n\\[\nR'\\cap M=\\mathbb C1.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>This is the cited irreducible hyperfinite embedding theorem of Popa. The special case \\(N=M\\) is [12, Corollary 4.1]; it is also used explicitly in [19, proof of Theorem 3.8]. The relative form stated here is recalled in [16, Introduction].</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are self-adjoint \\(a,b\\in R\\) such that \\( R=W^*(a,b). \\)", "statement": "<p>**Two self-adjoint generators for the hyperfinite factor — original Lemma 5.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For this lemma, \\(R\\) is the hyperfinite type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) factor with separable predual.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are self-adjoint \\(a,b\\in R\\) such that\n\\[\nR=W^*(a,b).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>By uniqueness of the hyperfinite type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) factor with separable predual [15, Section 1.3], use its standard tensor model\n\\[\nR=\\overline{\\bigotimes}_{m\\geq1}\n(M_2(\\mathbb C),\\operatorname{tr}_2).\n\\]\nOn the \\(m\\)-th tensor site, let\n\\[\nd_m=\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n1&amp;0\\\\\n0&amp;0\n\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad\nq_m=\\frac12\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n1&amp;1\\\\\n1&amp;1\n\\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\nwith the identity on the other sites. On that site,\n\\[\n2d_mq_m(1-d_m)=e_{12},\n\\]\nso \\(d_m,q_m\\) generate the full matrix algebra. Each of the two families \\((d_m)\\) and \\((q_m)\\) consists of commuting projections.</p><p>For any countable family of commuting projections \\((e_m)\\), the operator norm convergent series\n\\[\nb=\\sum_{m\\geq1}2\\,3^{-m}e_m\n\\]\nrecovers every \\(e_m\\) by spectral calculus. Inductively, after recovering \\(e_1,\\ldots,e_{m-1}\\), form\n\\[\nr_m=b-\\sum_{j&lt;m}2\\,3^{-j}e_j.\n\\]\nThe restriction of \\(r_m\\) to \\(1-e_m\\) lies between \\(0\\) and \\(3^{-m}\\), whereas its restriction to \\(e_m\\) lies between \\(2\\,3^{-m}\\) and \\(3\\,3^{-m}\\). Since \\(e_m\\) commutes with \\(r_m\\),\n\\[\ne_m\n=\n1_{((3/2)3^{-m},\\,\\infty)}(r_m)\n\\in W^*(b).\n\\]\nApplying this encoding separately to \\((d_m)\\) and \\((q_m)\\) gives two self-adjoint elements whose generated von Neumann algebra contains every tensor site, hence is all of \\(R\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\(M_2(N)\\) is generated by two self-adjoint elements.", "statement": "<p>**Two-by-two matrix encoding — original Lemma 5.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Here \\(N\\) is an arbitrary unital von Neumann algebra; no factoriality, finiteness, or separability assumption is required.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(N\\) is generated by four self-adjoint elements.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\(M_2(N)\\) is generated by two self-adjoint elements.</p>", "proof": "<p>Write\n\\[\nN=W^*(a_1,a_2,a_3,a_4),\n\\qquad a_j=a_j^*,\n\\]\nand choose\n\\[\nC&gt;\\|a_1\\|+\\|a_2\\|.\n\\]\nPut\n\\[\nA=\n\\begin{pmatrix}\na_1&amp;0\\\\\n0&amp;a_2+C1_N\n\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad\nB=\n\\begin{pmatrix}\na_3&amp;1_N\\\\\n1_N&amp;a_4\n\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad\nQ=W^*(A,B).\n\\]\nLet \\(E_{ij}\\) be the standard matrix units of \\(M_2(N)\\).</p><p>The first diagonal block of \\(A\\) has spectrum in\n\\[\n[-\\|a_1\\|,\\|a_1\\|],\n\\]\nand the second has spectrum in\n\\[\n[C-\\|a_2\\|,C+\\|a_2\\|].\n\\]\nFor\n\\[\n\\|a_1\\|&lt;\\lambda&lt;C-\\|a_2\\|,\n\\]\nspectral calculus therefore gives\n\\[\nE_{11}=1_{(-\\infty,\\lambda)}(A)\\in Q,\n\\qquad\nE_{22}=1-E_{11}\\in Q.\n\\]\nThe off-diagonal cuts of \\(B\\) give\n\\[\nE_{12}=E_{11}BE_{22}\\in Q,\n\\qquad\nE_{21}=E_{12}^*\\in Q.\n\\]\nThe first corner then contains\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\na_1E_{11}&amp;=E_{11}AE_{11},\n&amp;\na_2E_{11}&amp;=E_{12}AE_{21}-CE_{11},\\\\\na_3E_{11}&amp;=E_{11}BE_{11},\n&amp;\na_4E_{11}&amp;=E_{12}BE_{21}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThus \\(NE_{11}\\subset Q\\). Multiplying this corner by the matrix units yields\n\\[\nNE_{ij}\\subset Q\n\\]\nfor every \\(i,j\\), hence \\(Q=M_2(N)\\).</p>"}], "edges": [[1, 2, "cite"], [1, 3, "cite"], [1, 4, "cite"], [5, 10, "cite"], [6, 7, "cite"], [7, 8, "cite"], [8, 5, "cite"], [9, 5, "cite"], [10, 12, "cite"], [11, 6, "cite"], [11, 10, "cite"], [12, 0, "cite"], [12, 15, "cite"], [13, 11, "cite"], [13, 14, "cite"], [14, 12, "cite"], [15, 1, "cite"], [16, 13, "cite"], [16, 15, "cite"], [17, 14, "cite"], [17, 15, "cite"], [18, 1, "cite"]]});
